- op: discuss  # c62b
  at: '2026-07-29T17:00:11Z'  # c62b
  by: agent  # c62b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c62b
  clock: 8  # c62b
  nonce: c62b  # c62b
  message: "UNBLOCKED by the WI-talos owner decision (2026-07-29). This item was parked\npending \"a coordinated stable_id-scheme decision\"; that decision now exists and\nthis item's placement in it is settled.\n\nPREMISE RE-VERIFIED before deciding: all 5 sites still emit `stable_id=symbol_id`\n— bitbake.py:192, :302, :342, :382 (4 sites, matching the earlier correction to\nthe filed discussion, NOT the 2 the original text listed) and bibtex.py:125.\nThe defect is unchanged: a \"stable\" id that embeds path and line numbers is not\nstable, since it moves whenever the file does.\n\n    should be   sha256:9833bc15f983fc00\n    emits       bitbake:recipes/foo.bb:12-14:FOO:variable\n\nDECISION -- this fix ships as its OWN scheme bump, v10, AFTER the WI-talos\nrelabel ships as v9. Two bumps, so each release makes exactly one claim:\n\n    v9   relabel only          digest_compatible_with = v8   (unqualified)\n    v10  this fix              no compatibility declared\n\nWHY NOT fold it into v9: v9's whole value to a consumer is the promise \"the\nlabel changed, your identities did not, do not re-key.\" For bitbake and bibtex\nsymbols that promise would be FALSE — their stable_id is not being relabeled, it\nis being replaced by a real hash, so those identities genuinely change. Folding\nthe two together would force either a compatibility field carrying a per-producer\nexception list, or dropping the compatibility claim for stable_id entirely and\npoisoning the don't-re-key signal for every consumer on account of a 5-site\nexception in two niche formats. The owner chose to pay two rounds of churn to\nkeep both claims unconditional.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: this item stays OPEN until v9 has shipped. It is no longer blocked\non a DECISION — it is blocked on a RELEASE. Do not land it early; landing it\nbefore or with v9 breaks v9's unqualified compatibility claim, which is the\nentire reason v9 is worth shipping separately.\n\nWHEN IT LANDS, the mechanical work is already scoped by this item: route all 5\nsites through make_doc_stable_id (the same factory the T0 batch used in PR #808),\npreserving 1:1 distinctness by folding span/kind where same-name siblings exist.\nKeep node.id byte-identical — the minimal stable_id-only port, NOT\nmake_doc_symbol_ids, which would additionally add a start_line to the line-less\nbitbake node.ids. Flip the per-analyzer stable_id tests to assert\n`startswith(\"hgstableid10:\")` and `id != stable_id`, matching what\ntest_ini/test_requirements/test_properties do for the T0 batch."  # c62b
- op: discuss  # dac8
  at: '2026-07-30T07:13:27Z'  # dac8
  by: agent  # dac8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dac8
  clock: 9  # dac8
  nonce: dac8  # dac8
  message: "SCOPE CLARIFICATION (no status change) -- T1 is BOTH ids for the same five sites, not just stable_id.\n\nWhile draining INV-dulah's doc/config-family node.id limbs (PR #78, dev d0e45ce33b), the five T1 sites were re-verified live: bitbake.py 192/302/342/382 + bibtex.py 125 -- exactly the set WI-talos Ruling 3 enumerates, independently reached by grepping for hand-rolled id f-strings across all analyzers.\n\nThe finding worth recording: those five set 'stable_id = symbol_id'. The composite node.id IS their stable identity, so the node.id grammar fix and the stable_id fix are THE SAME EDIT and cannot be sequenced apart. That is precisely why PR #78 folded ini/requirements/properties (whose stable_id already came from make_doc_stable_id, independent of node.id, so their node.id was safe to correct now) while deliberately leaving bitbake/bibtex alone -- touching their node.id would have changed stable_id and falsified v9's unqualified digest-compatibility claim.\n\nSo T1 remains blocked on the RELEASE boundary per Ruling 3, and its scope should be read as 'node.id AND stable_id for these five sites', which is a single change rather than two."  # dac8
